@yurnov there's quite a bunch of things like this one. Some we tend to leave them as-is until there is a paper cut.

If we do ... do this to projects we use, what will end up happening is folks will start using it more than they would and we will have to hold the bag for that.

Do drop by #k8s-code-organization, start a thread there. There's a lot more way more pressing than this one for sure.

@liyuerich yes, with one change: claim `NetworkPolicy` rather than IPBlock. `IPBlock` is a struct inside `NetworkPolicySpec`, and the unit here is the kind, same as the other PRs in the table.

NetworkPolicy's six beta rules are all in `networking/v1`, and only one has a handwritten twin: the `cidr` required check at `pkg/apis/networking/validation/validation.go:249`. When you delete it, keep the early return on an empty `cidr`. Otherwise the empty string falls through to `IsValidCIDRForLegacyField` and reports a second error. `IngressClass` (6) and `IPAddress` (8) are still open in the group.

@datayurei yes, one PR, separate from #142151. `Scale` is its own type. The two rules on `autoscaling/v1` `ScaleSpec` and the four on `apps/v1beta1` and `apps/v1beta2` `ScaleSpec` are the same `replicas` minimum, and all of them pair with the one handwritten check in `ValidateScale` (`pkg/apis/autoscaling/validation/validation.go:50`). Tests already live under `test/declarative_validation/{autoscaling,apps}/scale`. That also settles the `apps` row I had as "needs a look first". It's yours if you want it.

@antcybersec #142333 is on the table for `certificates`. The alpha wave question isn't mine to answer. @yongruilin you did #140166, do you want 1.38 alpha to beta as one PR again or tracked per type?

Table refreshed at `451ab1d9b7c`. Total is 549, down from 574, after #141967 (StorageClass) and #142119 (RuntimeClass) merged. The last two `core` rules are on ReplicationController's `metav1.ObjectMeta`, so that row is out of scope now, same as `lifecycle`.

I reproduced the fractional-byte warning issue mentioned in the Sep 8 comment in unit tests: integer quantities above MaxInt64 are incorrectly reported as fractional because MilliValue() saturates before the % 1000 check.

I have a small local prototype using Quantity.AsScale(0), with regression coverage for Pod container resources, Pod overhead, and PVC storage. The new unit tests fail on master and both affected packages pass with the prototype. I have not reproduced this in a live cluster.

@thc1006, are you already working on this item? If not, I would be happy to take it.

Two concrete points from checking the current code and PR diffs.

@6democratickim9, the warning case starts earlier than a byte quantity exceeding MaxInt64: it is the conversion to millibytes that saturates. `9223372036854775` bytes still fits through `MilliValue()`, while `9223372036854776` bytes saturates to MaxInt64 and leaves a remainder of 807. Both are whole-byte quantities. Those two make a useful boundary pair alongside the larger inputs.

For the `AsScale(0)` change, I would cover all eight warning sites: container memory/ephemeral-storage requests and limits, the two overhead fields, and PVC storage requests and limits. The tests should check that actual fractions still warn, include sub-milli fractions, and exercise both the integer and `ToDec()` paths. The returned bool there means exactness; `AsMilliInt64` and `AsScaledInt64` report overflow, not whether rounding lost a fractional part. That distinction matters for this check.

@Octopusjust @thc1006, #142112 and #141328 currently both replace the same `allocated[i] + podRequests[i]` in `calculateNodeAllocatableRequest`, with separate saturating-add helpers. Please agree which PR carries that hunk and rebase the other after it lands. The event comparisons and the other scoring changes in #142112 still need their own review.

For closing this tracker, @jpbetz, I suggest requiring each remaining item to have either a merged fix with regression coverage or an explicit decision to defer it to a linked follow-up with an owner. In particular, the unchanged-PVC update case raised on September 9 still needs a recorded compatibility decision and a test for the chosen behavior; #141348's merge does not by itself document that decision. That would give us a concrete exit criterion for the remaining work.

@semx Let me be clearer about why the hold is there.

The hold has a reason. not only the EasyCLA problem and also the both PR's commits list Claude as Co-Authored-By, which the AI guidance in pull-requests.md does not allow. 

I use exclamation marks bcuz I was confuse , his contributions are too many fundamental problems and create PR in short time.

Proposed division of work:

@semx Yes, You can open PR against the `PodTemplate` cases and cover the pod update, ephemeral-container, resize, Node and PVC status paths.

@thc1006 Yes, you can take the LimitRanger and LimitRange ratio cases, and the DRA duplicate-key and roundUpRange cases.

I'll claim the the Cmp and String cases at scale MinInt32 cases.

@vishals-3 #142265 and #142266 are welcome. PTAL at the presubmit errors. Co-Authored-By has restrictions.

Also, clearly there is some use of AI in these PRs, to ensure we are following CNCF guidelines, please fill out the section in the PR templates about AI use accurately.  Also, please confirm to me here that you are checking the AI work before submitting PRs and responding to comments. The CNCF requires this. If you are unable fully review all the PRs and comments generated with the aid of AI, please let me know.

@jpbetz Thank you for spelling out the split, and for raising the AI question directly. That is a fair thing to ask. I will gladly take the LimitRanger and LimitRange ratio cases and the two DRA groups, starting with LimitRanger.

Yes, I check the work. I read every change before a push, the tests and linters run locally first, and I decide what goes into a PR. The AI usage section on #141327, #141328 and #141306 describes how the assistant is used. It drafts code and text for me, including replies such as this one, and I review and edit before anything is posted. The guidelines put the responsibility on the author, and that is how I treat it.

I know you have put a lot of time into these reviews, and I have been moving quickly this week. I will slow the pace to what I can review with care. If you would prefer the commit messages rewritten by hand, I will do that. Whatever works best for you and the other reviewers is fine with me.

@jpbetz PR up: #142323 — covers the pod update, ephemeral-container, resize, PodTemplate, Node and PVC status paths, plus two bugs the ratchet design itself would have shipped with (Node's update path calls both `ValidateNode` and `ValidateNodeUpdate` and appends both error lists, so the create-path call needed the stored set too or it silently re-rejected what the update-path call had just ratcheted; and the stored set was originally keyed by resource name alone, which would let an unchanged value in one container wave through a genuinely new occurrence of the same value in a different container). Both are covered by tests that go through the real registry strategy, not just the validation-package unit tests.

On checking the work: I look at the actual code before deciding a diagnosis or a fix is right, then use an assistant for a second pass and for drafting — text like this reply included — and read that back against the source before anything gets posted or pushed. I'm the one deciding what ships.

One open item: Node `capacity`/`allocatable` is in #142323, but RuntimeClass `overhead` from the same bullet isn't claimed by anyone in the split above. It's a small, separate fix (`ValidateRuntimeClassUpdate` and the overhead check already take the old object directly, no options plumbing needed) — happy to pick it up if it's still unclaimed, or leave it if you'd rather someone else take it.

@itzPranshul We're currently backlogged so if you work on it, you might not get a review any time soon.

The problem I've hit is that there has been heavy use of AI coding assistents to help with this issue, and the current generations of LLMs are goal seeking and are not getting to the bottom of the systemic problems of the quantity type very well. As a result, even with my best attempts are careful review, we've regressed behavior compared with 1.37.

For now, I'm focusing on by-hand fixes to make quantity's receiver functions immutable which I will then use to simplify implementation and make it easier to make stable progress here. I don't plan to spend time reviewing PRs until I feel caught up and things are in better shape.  Help with review welcome, but only if all comments are human curated (AI is fine to use, but read and understand ALL comments before posting please).

@jpbetz Yes, and "master passes" was a useless sentence on my part, sorry: those entries are pinned to what master does today, and passing says nothing about them. I did run the 35 inputs themselves on v1.37.0 and on master.

On v1.37.0 the zero entries parse as 0, and on master they fail with `ErrSuffix`. [#142452](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/142452) gives the 0 back.

The other 29 never return from `ParseQuantity` on v1.37.0 (two seconds each, and one I left running for five minutes). Master accepts all 29. 26 come back with the exponent wrapped, 3 with the right value (`1000000000000000000e-2147483649` is `1e-2147483631`). When their `String()` is parsed again, 25 hang and 4 come back, the four with a fraction and exponent -2147483647 (`9223372036854775.807e-2147483647` turns into `9223372036854775807e2147483646`).
